# /etc/installerconfig, for FreeBSD's bsdinstall in script mode.
#
# TWO PARTS, AND THE SEPARATOR IS THE `#!` LINE. Everything above it is the preamble,
# read as sh by the installer to set how the disk is prepared; everything below it is a
# script run under chroot in the new system before bsdinstall exits.
#
# WHY THIS IS THE WHOLE INSTALLER
#
# bsdinstall's `auto` target is a menu wizard. `script` is the same steps driven from
# this file - and it is not a subset: the manual lists the two as identical paths, with
# script mode skipping the prompts. So a bake needs no screen, no coordinates and no
# driver, which is why BSD is closer to the Ubuntu answer-file model than to MX's.
#
# ZFS, AND THE REASON IT CHANGED IS THE ONLY REASON THAT WOULD HAVE CHANGED IT.
#
# This used UFS, and the note here argued for it: a pool's metadata and its ARC make a disk
# larger and slower to compress, and a throwaway VM gains nothing from copy-on-write. That
# argument is still true and it is not the whole picture:
#
#     UFS has no snapshot and no trim of its own. The clean stage ran `fsck_ffs -E`, which
#     walks the cylinder groups and discards the free ones - and then the image came out
#     barely smaller, because UFS keeps its free space as a bitmap of blocks it once wrote.
#
# ZFS can free blocks without being told which ones: `zpool trim` makes the pool tell the
# device that every unallocated block is unallocated, and `discard=unmap` on the qemu drive
# drops the clusters. That is the mechanism this repository already relies on for Linux, and
# FreeBSD was the one system where it did not exist.
#
# WHAT IT COSTS: a pool label, the MOS, and an ARC that wants memory. On a 2 GiB guest that
# is a few hundred MiB of disk and about 100 MiB of RAM. The trade is worth it if the trim
# releases gigabytes, and that is measured by the run rather than assumed here.
#
# THE SCRIPTED FORM IS ZFSBOOT_DISKS, NOT A MENU. Setting it makes bsdinstall's zfsboot take
# its non-interactive path - f_interactive is false, so the layout menu is skipped entirely
# (see zfsboot: "Short-circuit if we're running non-interactively").
#
#     ZFSBOOT_DISKS=vtbd0          the single virtio disk this bake attaches
#     ZFSBOOT_VDEV_TYPE=stripe     one disk, no redundancy
#     ZFSBOOT_SWAP_SIZE=2g         zfsboot insists on a swap partition size
#     ZFSBOOT_POOL_NAME=zroot      the name FreeBSD's own installer uses
#
# NO SWAP IS ACTUALLY WANTED, and 2g is what the variable requires rather than what this
# needs. A 2 GiB guest with no hibernation does not swap, and the space comes back through
# the trim anyway.
ZFSBOOT_DISKS=vtbd0
ZFSBOOT_VDEV_TYPE=stripe
ZFSBOOT_POOL_NAME=zroot
ZFSBOOT_SWAP_SIZE=2g
export ZFSBOOT_DISKS ZFSBOOT_VDEV_TYPE ZFSBOOT_POOL_NAME ZFSBOOT_SWAP_SIZE

# NO DISTRIBUTIONS, BECAUSE THIS MEDIUM HAS NONE TO UNPACK.
#
# `DISTRIBUTIONS="kernel.txz base.txz"` was here and it is the reason every FreeBSD run
# stopped at mount_aux with the disk stuck at 193 MiB. 15.1's disc1 is a PKGBASE image:
#
#     % xorriso -indev 15_1_RELEASE_AMD64_CD.iso -find /usr/freebsd-dist
#     '/usr/freebsd-dist'
#     '/usr/freebsd-dist/MANIFEST'          <- a manifest, and no .txz beside it
#
# The payload is 330 packages under /usr/freebsd-packages/offline instead, with
# FreeBSD-set-base, FreeBSD-set-minimal, FreeBSD-kernel-generic and pkg among them.
#
# bsdinstall's script mode branches on exactly this - /usr/libexec/bsdinstall/script:
#
#     if [ -n "$COMPONENTS" -a -n "$DISTRIBUTIONS" ]; then error ...
#     elif [ -z "$DISTRIBUTIONS" ]; then bsdinstall pkgbase --non-interactive
#     else   exec 5>&1; BSDINSTALL_DISTDIR=$(fetchmissingdists) ...
#
# and fetchmissingdists goes to the mirror when a named set is not on the medium:
#
#     if [ ! -f $BSDINSTALL_DISTDIR/$dist ]; then FETCH_DISTRIBUTIONS="..."
#     ...
#     BSDINSTALL_DISTSITE=$(mirrorselect)      <- an interactive dialog
#
# Nothing answers that dialog. The run sat in it for fifty-two minutes - the console's
# last line was "Running installation step: mount_aux", because mount_aux and
# fetchmissingdists print nothing while they work, and the disk held at 193 MiB with
# the partitions made and the filesystem mounted and not one file unpacked into it.
#
# Leaving the variable unset takes the pkgbase branch, which is what this image was built
# for. COMPONENTS names what to install from it; the base set pulls in minimal, devel and
# optional, so it is the whole system, and the generic kernel is separate in pkgbase.
#
#    base        everything that was in base.txz - minimal, devel, optional
#    kernel-dbg  the debug kernel symbols, which 15.1's disc offers by default
#
# NO DISTRIBUTIONS AND NO COMPONENTS IS ALSO AN ANSWER: script mode then calls pkgbase
# with no COMPONENTS, which selects "base" and "kernel-dbg" itself (pkgbase.in, the
# non_interactive branch). Naming them is doing that deliberately rather than relying on
# a default, and the debug symbols are 130 MiB that this disk does not need.
#
# BSDINSTALL_PKG_REPOS_DIR IS WHAT POINTS pkgbase AT THE MEDIUM. pkgbase.in defaults it
# to /usr/share/bsdinstall, which holds only a network repository (FreeBSD-base.conf,
# pkg+https://pkg.FreeBSD.org). The offline repository is
# /usr/freebsd-packages/repos/FreeBSD-base-offline.conf, and bsdinstall's interactive
# path sets exactly this variable when the user chooses "Offline (Limited Packages)".
# Setting it in the preamble is the same choice, made once.
#
# AND BOTH HAVE TO BE EXPORTED. /usr/libexec/bsdinstall/pkgbase is a flua script, not a
# shell function: it runs as a child process and reads these through os.getenv(). A
# variable set but not exported in this preamble is invisible to it, and the failure
# would be silent - pkgbase would fall back to /usr/share/bsdinstall's network
# repository and try to reach pkg.FreeBSD.org from a guest that has an offline CD.
COMPONENTS="base"
BSDINSTALL_PKG_REPOS_DIR="/usr/freebsd-packages/repos/"
export COMPONENTS
export BSDINSTALL_PKG_REPOS_DIR

# THE ROOT PASSWORD, PLAIN, BECAUSE THE INSTALLER WANTS IT THAT WAY. It accepts a
# crypt(3) string through ROOTPASS_ENC as well; plain is used here because the hash has to
# match `admin` and a literal in the file is checkable by eye.
ROOTPASS_PLAIN=admin

# NO SWAP PARTITION. PARTITIONS=DEFAULT would add one; a VM with 2 GiB of RAM and no
# hibernation does not need it, and it is disk that has to be compressed and shipped.

# nonInteractive=YES IS REQUIRED BY THE ZFS PATH, AND THE COMMENT THAT USED TO BE HERE
# SAID THE OPPOSITE. It argued that the variable belonged to the ZFS path, that setting
# it here was ignored, and that script mode had no prompts to silence. The first clause
# was right and that is exactly why it has to be set: bsdinstall's script target runs
# `bsdinstall zfsboot` as a child, and zfsboot decides whether to draw its menu with
#
#     while :; do
#         if ! f_interactive; then
#             retval=$DIALOG_OK
#             mtag=">>> $msg_install"      <- non-interactive: take the default and go
#         else
#             dialog_menu_main             <- interactive: draw a menu, wait for a key
#             retval=$?
#         fi
#
# and f_interactive (usr.sbin/bsdconfig/share/variable.subr) is
#
#     f_interactive() { ! f_getvar $VAR_NONINTERACTIVE value || [ ! "$value" ]; }
#
# with VAR_NONINTERACTIVE = "nonInteractive". So the ONLY thing that makes zfsboot take
# the first branch is this variable having a value. ZFSBOOT_DISKS does not: it makes the
# layout step non-interactive once the main menu has been passed (zfsboot's own note
# says "dialog_menu_layout supports running non-interactively"), but the main menu runs
# before that.
#
# WHAT THAT COST. Run 41985197417 set ZFSBOOT_DISKS and not this variable, so zfsboot
# drew the menu. bsddialog takes its geometry from the terminal, resizewin could not
# measure a raw serial pipe, every dimension came back 0, and the computed row count
# came back -6:
#
#     resizewin: timeout reading from terminal
#     f_dialog_max_size: bsddialog --print-maxsize = [MaxSize: 0, 0]
#     f_getvar: var=[rows] value=[-6]
#     bsddialog: invalid option -- 6
#     retval=254 mtag=[bsddialog: invalid option -- 6]
#
# zfsboot then f_die'd on retval 254, startbsdinstall tried to show the error in another
# bsddialog with the same 0x0 geometry, and its own copy of the menu loop is what the
# console sat in for twenty-five minutes with the disk at 0 MiB. The installer was not
# slow; it was waiting for a key on a screen that had no size.
#
# THE UFS PATH NEVER HIT THIS because `bsdinstall scriptedpart` reads PARTITIONS and
# does not draw anything, which is why every earlier run got as far as pkgbase.
#
# It is exported because zfsboot is a child process, and lowercase because that is the
# name the variable table uses - `f_getvar $VAR_NONINTERACTIVE` reads "nonInteractive",
# not "NONINTERACTIVE".
nonInteractive=YES
export nonInteractive

#!/bin/sh
# The post-install script is fetched rather than inlined, so the same file can be read and
# tested on its own. bsd/freebsd/bake.py replaces the placeholder below with the contents
# of installerconfig.sh at bake time, and refuses to start if it is missing - a preamble
# with no setup script installs a system with no ssh, which is a failure that only shows
# up in the next stage.
#
# THE PLACEHOLDER IS SPELLED OUT ONCE, AND THAT MATTERS.
#
# The comment above used to contain the token literally, to name it. bake.py substitutes
# with str.replace, which replaces EVERY occurrence - so the token inside this comment was
# replaced too, and the comment's tail became live script:
#
#     # tested on its own. ... replaces the #!/bin/sh
#     # the contents of installerconfig.sh at bake time, and refuses to start if the
#       marker is
#     # missing - a preamble with no setup script ...
#
# The middle line is the damage: `marker is` is no longer a comment, so the chroot ran it
# and printed `/tmp/installscript: marker: not found`. It was harmless to the install and
# it looked like a real failure in the console, which is worse than either.
@POST_INSTALL@
